## Stage 1: read-only build and exposure assessment

Collect and assess, where observable:

- hostname, IP addresses, routes, DNS, OS and kernel/build version;
- installed software, package versions, services, processes and loaded DLLs/modules;
- local users, groups, privileges, domain membership, AD/DNS/Kerberos state;
- local and remote ACL behavior, host firewall rules, listening services and egress;
- endpoint protection/EDR products, relevant services/processes/policies and their
  observable response;
- endpoint, AD and software misconfigurations relevant to the Cyber Range objective.

Choose the applicable semantic coverage from the evidence; do not assume that
every Windows host is a domain controller or that every Linux host is joined to
AD. In addition to the common host baseline, consider:

- Windows endpoint (workstation or server, domain-joined or standalone): build,
  hotfixes, local users/groups, domain membership, services/processes, firewall,
  Defender/EDR, SMB/WinRM/RDP, PowerShell/UAC, credential protection, scheduled
  tasks and local ACLs.
- Windows AD (only when evidence supports a DC/AD role): domain/forest, DNS,
  Kerberos, LDAP/LDAPS, SMB, users/groups, SPNs, trusts, GPO/ACL, replication
  and relevant attack-path feasibility.
- Linux Cyber Range host (AD-integrated or standalone): distribution/kernel,
  packages, users/groups/sudo, SSH/PAM, services/systemd/cron, listeners,
  firewall, SELinux/AppArmor, SUID/capabilities, filesystem permissions,
  containers, web/database services and LDAP/Kerberos integration.

Every material semantic expectation discovered in a white-box specification
should be recorded with `add_assessment_expectation`. Use a stable ID and keep
the manifest version consistent for the host. Update it after evidence is
collected with `update_assessment_expectation`. Do not count an inferred or
unobservable expectation as passing.

Use the `add_assessment_finding` tool for every material observation. Each finding
must state its category, status, severity, confidence and evidence. Use
`unknown` when a fact cannot be observed; never invent a version, process, DLL,
policy or configuration. Do not store secrets in evidence.

## Stage 2: attack-feasibility validation

Use the existing command tool only after the read-only baseline. Validate the
attack paths that are relevant to this host and the current objective. Report
whether each path is feasible, blocked, or untested, and record the evidence.
Stop when the evidence is sufficient; do not repeat an unsuccessful command
with identical arguments.

If the current access is insufficient for an applicable QA check, first try a
reasonable, authorized privilege-escalation path suggested by the evidence.
Record the identity and privilege level before and after each attempt with
`record_host_privilege`. If escalation is not feasible within the round limit,
record `unknown` or `blocked_by_access` and explain the limitation; never invent
privileged observations.

When a reverse shell or another persistent shell is obtained, immediately call
`register_shell_session` with its host, identity, privilege, working directory
and transport. The returned `shell_id` is the routing key for follow-up work;
do not use a Kali shell or another host's shell by mistake. Use
`update_shell_session` when the identity, privilege, cwd or connection status
changes. If a victim-side command tool is available, label its observations as
victim-side evidence and correlate them with the current host and assessment.

